  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA low will continue to move up the east coast this morning,\nleading to increased chances of rain, mainly over southern New\nHampshire, coastal Maine, and into portions of the Interior. The\ntrends for a low track further out to sea have held serve and we\ncontinue to expect the highest totals will end up mainly along\nthe Maine coast. The highest NBM probabilities for at least an\ninch have pretty much moved out to sea. Along the immediate\ncoast, we now only see about a 20 to 50 percent chance of an\ninch or more. The highest probabilities for a half inch or more\ndon't penetrate much farther inland, but we do see some 50 to \n70 percent values along the coast and into portions of the \nInterior. The best guess suggests that most areas will see under\na quarter inch of rain but some readings in the quarter to half\ninch range will remain possible along the coast with values in \nthe half inch to an inch range across the Midcoast and vicinity.\nThis system will be quick to move off to the northeast with \nmost of the rain wrapping up early to mid afternoon. We should\nalso see some clearing south of the mountains. \n\nA cold front will cross the forecast area through the day which\ncould lead to some hit or miss showers in the mountains. This \ntiming will also mean a mild day for most, especially for those \nsouth of the mountains. Highs will range from the low to mid 50s\nnorth, to the low to mid 60s south and along the coast.\nTemperatures will be chilly tonight behind the front, mainly\nranging from the lower 20s north to the lower 30s south.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nQuiet weather will dominate the pattern Tuesday, Wednesday, and\nmuch of Thanksgiving Day. Highs will mainly be in the lower 30s\nto lower 40s during the afternoons. We could see widespread \nlows in the teens Wednesday and Thursday mornings. \n\nThe next coastal storm then approaches late in the day on\nThursday. Depending on your model/ensemble member of choice, we\nwill start seeing increasing precipitation chances either late\nin the afternoon or at night. The bulk of the precipitation will\nhang around through the day on Friday and move out either \nFriday night or Saturday morning. While there is still \nsignificant spread regarding the eventual low track among the \nvarious global ensembles, there does continue to be a notable \ntrend for a more inland track. This would mean a warmer system \nwith stronger easterly/onshore winds. Thus, we could still see \nsome winter like conditions, but mainly over the higher \nelevations. A wintry mix with sleet and freezing rain is also \nnot out of the question for some areas in and around the \nFoothills and Interior.\n\nGiven the trends for strong onshore flow, coastal flooding\nis perhaps the biggest concern from this storm as far as \nimpacts go. High astronomical tides are predicted to overlap \nduring this time period. Additionally, strong easterly winds \ncould lead to a limited power outage threat.\n\nIt does appear that we will see a much more widespread soaking\nrain from this low, but as we have seen with the low currently \nmoving up the coast, there is more than enough time for models \nto shift over the coming days so confidence remains rather low.",
